WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.900
Integrity evidence helps prove that the artifact being used is the artifact that was reviewed or approved.

2
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:17.240
Without that evidence, a team may trust a model name while the actual file has changed, moved, been replaced, or been rebuilt in ways nobody recorded.

3
00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:23.680
Hashes and checksums identify exact files and help detect accidental or unauthorized changes.

4
00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:31.940
They are especially useful when artifacts are copied between storage systems, cached in deployment environments, or referenced by automation.

5
00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:37.880
A hash does not prove a model is good, but it helps prove which model file is under discussion.

6
00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:47.040
Signatures and attestations can connect artifacts to trusted producers, build processes, release workflows, or approval gates.

7
00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:54.740
They can also help future teams understand whether a checkpoint came from a controlled process or from an informal copy.

8
00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:58.680
The value comes from connecting the technical evidence to an operating policy.

9
00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:05.680
Artifact registries and controlled storage reduce unapproved substitution, mystery copies, and uncontrolled drift.

10
00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:11.940
A shared environment should not depend on whichever checkpoint happens to be present on a local disk.

11
00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:17.480
Approved artifacts should have known locations, access rules, and promotion history.

12
00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:24.240
Integrity checks should be tied to deployment and promotion workflows, not performed only once during a manual download.

13
00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:33.300
Store evidence with enough context that future maintainers can understand what was checked, who checked it, what approval it supported, and why it mattered for the workflow.

14
00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:36.460
The evidence should also be easy to compare.

15
00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:48.240
If two teams claim to use the same model, hashes, registry references, and attestation records should make it possible to confirm whether they are truly using the same artifact or only the same friendly name.
